// Operand evaluation order, isolated. A side effect sitting in an
// operand position happens in whatever order the target evaluates
// operands in, and `ocaml` is the one target that goes the other way:
//
//   emo run main.emo           -> 12 = 3
//   build --target ocaml       -> 21 = 3
//   build --target c / typescript / beam / wasm   -> 12 = 3
//
// The value is the same everywhere — only the trace differs — which is
// what makes it worth a probe: nothing fails, and a program that hands
// out ids, counters or event tags from an operand position answers
// differently on one target. `c`, `typescript` and `beam` were each run
// against this program when the finding was recorded; `check.sh` pins
// the interpreter and `ocaml`, because they are the disagreement.
//
// The cause is the emitted source, not a decision: `emo_codegen.ml`
// writes a call as `func [a1; a2]` and OCaml evaluates a constructor's
// arguments right to left, so the host language's rule reaches Emo's.
// The reversal covers all five operand positions — a call's arguments,
// an array literal's elements, a binary operator's operands, an
// interpolation's parts, and named arguments, each of which the recorded
// finding lists. `CHECK.md` records it; `golden/main.emo` takes its tags
// in statements of their own so no operand position can decide them.

def note(trace Box[String], i Int64) Int64 {
  trace.replace(trace.read() + "${i}")
  return i
}

def two(a Int64, b Int64) Int64 {
  return a + b
}

def main() {
  const trace = Box.new("")
  const sum = two(note(trace, 1), note(trace, 2))
  println("${trace.read()} = ${sum}")
}

main()
